{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And I, behold I, am bringing the flood — water.</b> The reason for the punishment of the generation of the flood is that they sinned in idolatry, in sexual immorality, and in robbery. For concerning idolatry it is written (Jeremiah 2:13): ״אֹתִי עָזְבוּ מְקוֹר מַיִם חַיִּים לַחְצֹב לָהֶם בֹּארוֹת בֹּארוֹת נִשְׁבָּרִים אֲשֶׁר לֹא יָכִלוּ הַמָּיִם״. For the sin of forbidden relations, because one who lies with another man's wife is executed by strangulation, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ketuvot 30a): ״Even though the four death-penalties of the court have been abolished, the law of the four death-penalties has not been abolished — one who was liable to strangulation [is punished] by drowning in a river״ and so forth. It follows that the generation of the flood, who stumbled with another man's wife, whose penalty is strangulation — it is fitting that they should drown in the water. And as it is written (Psalms 32:6): ״עַל זֹאת יִתְפַּלֵּל כָּל חָסִיד אֵלֶיךָ לְעֵת מְצֹא״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted ״לְעֵת מְצֹא״ as referring to a woman, and adjacent to it: ״רַק לְשֶׁטֶף מַיִם רַבִּים אֵלָיו לֹא יַגִּיעוּ״ — the implication being that when the wife is absent, since he does not escape from transgression, the rushing of many waters will reach him. For the sin of robbery, because every robber enters into another's territory, therefore it is fitting to bring upon them the rushing of many waters, for then every drop touches its fellow and enters into its boundary. For concerning rains of blessing it is stated (Job 38:25): ״מִי פִלַּג לַשֶּׁטֶף תְּעָלָה״, from which they learn (Bava Batra 16a) that every drop has its own mold, and there is the width of a hair between every drop and drop, so that one should not enter into the boundary of its fellow. But this robber who enters into another's territory — it is fitting that the rains of blessing be turned for him into a flood, for then all the drops intermingle." "And on this basis the first chapter of tractate Ta'anit (7a) concludes: Rabbi Ami said: the rains are withheld only on account of the sin of robbery, as it is stated (Job 36:32): ״עַל כַּפַּיִם כִּסָּה אוֹר״. For when the expression ״rains״ is used unqualifiedly, it speaks of rains of blessing, as Rashi explained: at first it is stated ״וַיְהִי הַגֶּשֶׁם עַל הָאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 7:12), and only afterward is it stated ״וַיְהִי הַמַּבּוּל״ (Genesis 7:10) — rather, when He brought them down, He brought them down with mercy, for perhaps they would repent and then they would be rains of blessing. And with this it is well explained that there was no need to say that Noah was one of the small of faith, for according to its plain sense this is not implied; and in any case he was uncertain about the coming of the flood, for perhaps they would repent, just as the prophet Jonah was uncertain about the prophecy of destruction to the people of Nineveh. For this reason it is stated ״מִפְּנֵי מֵי הַמַּבּוּל״ (Genesis 7:7) — but while they were still rains he did not enter the ark, for perhaps they would repent and they would be rains of blessing. Therefore he said: ״the rains״ — that is, rains of blessing — ״are withheld only on account of the sin of robbery,״ for this robber who enters into his fellow's boundary — it is not fitting that rains of blessing should come down for him, since one drop does not enter into the boundary of its fellow. And it seems proper in my eyes to say that Rabbi Ami is speaking about the generation of the flood, and his prooftext demonstrates it, for one must examine the verse ״עַל כַּפַּיִם כִּסָּה אוֹר״: why did he call robbery ״hands,״ and why did he call the rains ״light״? Rather, surely he interpreted this verse by juxtaposing it to what is said there: ״כִּי בָם יָדִין עַמִּים״ (Job 36:31). Hear from this that he is speaking about the generation of the flood, who were judged by water. And he spoke of robbery in terms of ״hands,״ because the Holy One, blessed be He, created each and every finger for its own function, as is found in the Gemara (Ketuvot 5a), and the Holy One, blessed be He, desired that no finger enter into the boundary of another; and this robber profanes the sanctity of his hands — therefore they were judged by the flood, for then the clouds covered the light of the sun, for the clouds were so thick and dense that sparks of sunlight could not pass through the clouds, since the luminaries did not function throughout all the days of the flood. And on account of the density of the clouds, the waters of the flood came down in foam — foaming, and the rains of blessing were withheld and the flood came upon them. This is what the verse says: ״עַל כַּפַּיִם כִּסָּה אוֹר״, and this idea is very precious."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:43.757712802Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}